senechal switched from direct-push-to-origin/main to a PR-based landing flow (2026-08-04, Zach-directed, see CLAUDE.md 'Landing changes: PR, not direct push'). Trigger: a nightly session committed locally, then found origin/main had moved out from under it mid-run -- a PR (#5) had squash-merged a commit (e93d702) already sitting at local main's tip, under a different sha. git rebase origin/main resolved it cleanly since git recognized the already-applied commit and skipped it, but that only worked because the divergence was noticed by hand before pushing -- a plain git push would have failed opaquely or, if force-resolved wrong, silently dropped/duplicated content. senechal now runs an explicit staleness/dirty-tree check (git fetch, git status clean, diff main vs origin/main, rebase+re-verify manifest if diverged) before opening any PR, same spirit as focus-commit's before/after manifest check but applied to the whole repo rather than just FOCUS.md/QUESTIONS.md. Flagging because other repos in this ecosystem likely carry the same 'Claude may push directly to origin/main' assumption senechal just retired, and this divergence failure mode (a squash-merged PR changing a commit's sha out from under a local branch that already has that same content) isn't specific to senechal -- worth considering as a fleet-wide protocol/command the way focus-commit and check-project-busy already are, rather than each project re-deriving its own staleness check by hand. Not fixing this myself since it's realisateur's domain to decide whether/how to standardize it -- filing it, not building it.
